import json

upsc = []

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 1: DISTRIBUTION
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about the distribution of the Arctic or Polar Climate, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Ice-caps are confined to Greenland and the highlands of high-latitude regions, where the ground is permanently snow-covered.\n2. Tundra vegetation occurs on lowlands with a few ice-free months, including coastal Greenland, northern Canada/Alaska, and the Arctic seaboard of Eurasia.\n3. Antarctica has only a thin, seasonal ice cover, melting completely each summer, unlike the permanent ice of Greenland.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect and reverses the actual fact — the text describes Antarctica as the greatest single stretch of PERMANENT ice-cap in the world, with ice layers as thick as 3,000 metres, not a thin, seasonally melting cover. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 2: TEMPERATURE
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about polar temperature, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["Despite the long duration of continuous summer sunshine (the 'midnight sun'), temperatures remain low because the sun is low in the sky and much of its faint warmth is reflected by snow or consumed melting ice","The warmest month (June) in polar regions seldom rises above about 10°C, with mid-winter temperatures as low as -37°C or colder in the interior","Normally more than eight months of the year have temperatures above freezing point, making the polar climate milder than commonly assumed","At the North Pole, there are six months without light in winter, corresponding to weeks of continuous darkness within the Arctic and Antarctic Circles generally"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual fact — the text explicitly states normally NOT MORE than four months have temperatures above freezing point, not more than eight; the polar climate is characterised by a very short thaw period, not sustained above-freezing conditions. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"According to Goh Cheng Leong, to what depth can summer heat typically thaw the frozen polar soil, leaving the rest permanently frozen (permafrost)?","o":["About 150 mm (6 inches)","About 1.5 metres (5 feet)","About 15 metres (50 feet)","The soil does not thaw at all, even in summer"],"a":0,"e":"The text explicitly states summer heat can only thaw the upper 150 mm (6 inches) of the soil, with the ground remaining solidly frozen (permafrost) for all but about four months and inaccessible to plants below that shallow depth — not 1.5 metres, 15 metres, or complete absence of thaw."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 3: PRECIPITATION
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about polar precipitation, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["Precipitation is heavy throughout the polar regions, generally exceeding 1,000 mm annually due to constant cyclonic activity","In regions dominated by winds from anticyclones over the ice-caps, rain tends to come in summer, when more evaporation is possible","In coastal areas more strongly influenced by cyclones, there is a tendency towards a winter maximum, since cyclonic activity is greatest then","Stations such as Angmagssalik can receive over 940 mm annually in areas where cyclones are especially frequent, well above the typical tundra range of 250-300 mm"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the general pattern — the text explicitly states precipitation in polar regions is generally LIGHT (not more than about 300 mm/year for most tundra locations), since convectional rainfall is largely absent due to low evaporation and dry, cold polar air; only specific cyclone-prone stations like Angmagssalik receive markedly higher totals. The other three statements are correct."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 4: NATURAL VEGETATION
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about tundra vegetation, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. There are no trees in the tundra mainly because the warmest month rarely exceeds the 10°C 'tree-survival line' and annual precipitation is very low.\n2. In more sheltered spots, stunted birches, dwarf willows and undersized alders manage to survive, though poorly developed.\n3. Reindeer moss provides an important food source for herbivorous animals such as reindeer in the coastal lowlands.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":0,"e":"All three statements are correct, matching the text's explanation of the tree-line threshold, the marginal survival of stunted trees in sheltered spots, and reindeer moss's role as tundra pasturage."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 5: HUMAN ACTIVITIES
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Match List-I (Nomadic/Indigenous Tribe) with List-II (Region, per Goh Cheng Leong) and select the correct answer using the code below.\nList-I:\nA. Eskimos\nB. Lapps\nC. Samoyeds\nD. Yakuts\nList-II:\n1. Lena basin\n2. Greenland, northern Canada and Alaska\n3. Ural Mountains and Yenisey basin\n4. Northern Finland and Scandinavia\nCodes:","o":["A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1","A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1","A-1, B-4, C-3, D-2","A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3"],"a":0,"e":"The correct match is A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1: Eskimos live in Greenland/northern Canada/Alaska; Lapps in northern Finland and Scandinavia; Samoyeds around the Ural Mountains and Yenisey basin; and Yakuts in the Lena basin — all named tundra-dwelling peoples described in the text."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about the Eskimos, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["The Eskimo population today numbers in the tens of millions, making them one of the most numerous indigenous groups in the world","The Polar Eskimos around Thule in north-west Greenland still lead a life not very different from their forefathers","During winter, some Eskimos live in compact igloos, moving to portable skin tents by streams in summer to hunt","Contact with Europeans over the last fifty years has brought major changes, including permanent wooden houses, speed-boats, and rifles replacing traditional tools"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect — the text explicitly states the Eskimo population numbers 'less than 28,000' at the time of writing, a very small figure, not tens of millions. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about recent economic development of the Arctic region, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Minerals mined in the Arctic include gold in Alaska, nickel near Petsamo (U.S.S.R.), petroleum in Alaska's Kenai Peninsula, and copper at Rankin Inlet, Canada.\n2. Declining iron ore reserves around Lake Superior have led Great Lakes industries to increasingly rely on iron ore from Labrador.\n3. The Arctic seaboard of Eurasia has become entirely unusable for shipping, since no modern technology has been able to overcome the permanent ice cover of its ports.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the text explicitly states that modern ice-breakers keep the passage open most of the time at ports like Igarka, enabling shipment of timber and fur from Siberia, not that the seaboard is entirely unusable. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 6: NCERT — KOEPPEN'S POLAR CLIMATES (E: TUNDRA ET, ICE CAP EF)
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about Koeppen's Polar Climates (Group E), per NCERT — which directly correspond to Goh Cheng Leong's 'Arctic or Polar Climate':\n1. Polar climates exist poleward beyond 70° latitude and consist of two types: Tundra (ET) and Ice Cap (EF).\n2. The Tundra climate is named after its characteristic vegetation of low-growing mosses, lichens and flowering plants, and is a region of permafrost.\n3. The Ice Cap climate (EF) occurs over interior Greenland and Antarctica, where temperatures remain below freezing point even in summer.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":0,"e":"All three statements are correct, matching NCERT's description of Koeppen's Polar Climates group, the Tundra (ET) type's defining vegetation and permafrost, and the Ice Cap (EF) type's location and perpetually sub-freezing character."})

upsc.append({"q":"Assertion (A): NCERT cites Plateau Station, Antarctica (79°S), as representative of the Ice Cap (EF) climate, corroborating Goh Cheng Leong's description of Antarctica's ice sheet.\nReason (R): Both sources describe accumulated ice and snow under mounting pressure causing deformation of ice sheets, which break apart and move as icebergs into surrounding waters.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above Assertion (A) and Reason (R)?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and R explains A — NCERT's account of ice-sheet deformation and iceberg calving under mounting ice pressure is precisely the mechanism underlying the vast, permanent Antarctic ice cap that Goh Cheng Leong independently describes as up to 3,000 metres thick, the greatest single stretch of ice-cap in the world."})

